# AI Plan & TODO — gemma.ps1

Branch: `work`
Repository: `E-ui-cell/mira`
Scope: `gemma/gemma.ps1`

## Goal

Keep Google Search behavior strictly provider-specific. Antigravity OAuth models must use Google's own native, server-side grounding. Gemini Developer API models must use the script's agentic `web_search` function, backed by the Antigravity OAuth Google Search endpoint, because the Gemini Developer API provider does not get that free Antigravity-native grounding.

## Required provider behavior

### Antigravity OAuth

- [x] Do not expose the script-defined/client-side `web_search` function.
- [x] Add native `googleSearch: {}` to the Antigravity model request when `.grounding on`.
- [x] Enable `toolConfig.includeServerSideToolInvocations = true` for Antigravity requests with native grounding and function calling.
- [ ] Test that the selected Antigravity model performs native Search internally when it decides Search is needed.
- [ ] Confirm that no `[Gemini tool call: web_search]` appears for Antigravity.
- [ ] Confirm that grounding-off requests omit native `googleSearch` and do not search.
- [ ] Inspect returned grounding metadata (queries, source titles/URLs) where available; do not mistake a model's answer or a tool-cycle STOP for proof of successful grounding.

### Gemini Developer API

- [x] Expose the script-defined `web_search` function only when provider is `gemini`, grounding is ON, and `MIRA_ENABLE_AGENTIC_WEB_SEARCH = $true`.
- [x] Keep native `googleSearch: {}` out of Gemini Developer API answer requests.
- [x] Route the agentic search function to the Antigravity OAuth endpoint, using the configured research model `MIRA_SEARCH_MODEL`.
- [ ] Test that a Gemini API model can decide to call `web_search`.
- [ ] Verify the Antigravity OAuth search response contains actual grounding metadata and source URLs.
- [ ] Verify the research result is returned to the Gemini API model and used in its final answer.
- [ ] Verify the tool is absent when `MIRA_ENABLE_AGENTIC_WEB_SEARCH = $false` or grounding is OFF.

## Diagnostics and reliability TODO

- [x] Log provider, selected answer model, and search mode clearly in `.grounding status`.
- [x] Keep native-grounding diagnostics distinct from client-side `web_search` execution diagnostics.
- [x] Surface grounding query strings, source count, source titles, and URLs when available; never claim a search succeeded without grounding metadata.
- [x] Preserve HTTP status and raw Google error response; additionally extract structured error reason, model, and retry delay when present. A 503 `MODEL_CAPACITY_EXHAUSTED` remains identified as a capacity issue, not an authentication or tool-configuration failure.
- [ ] Test both provider paths with grounding ON and OFF, including a request that does not need search.
- [ ] Run Windows PowerShell 5.1 syntax validation and live endpoint tests after changes; GitHub content verification alone is not runtime testing.

Implementation note: diagnostic wiring committed to `work` in `e9c7c4f3832b1da5405afbef586db39b1fd3e80d`. Native Antigravity responses now report grounding metadata when present and explicitly say when metadata is absent; Gemini API `web_search` reports its research model, query count, source count, queries, and source URLs. Structured Google API error details are printed in addition to the raw response. These are code changes only; no live provider or Windows PowerShell runtime test has been run.

## Acceptance matrix

| Provider / setting | Expected behavior |
|---|---|
| Antigravity OAuth + grounding ON | Native Google Search grounding; no client `web_search` function |
| Antigravity OAuth + grounding OFF | No native Search tool |
| Gemini Developer API + grounding ON + agentic search enabled | Client `web_search` function; search executed through Antigravity OAuth |
| Gemini Developer API + grounding ON + agentic search disabled | No `web_search` function |
| Gemini Developer API + grounding OFF | No `web_search` function |

## Constraints

- Work only on branch `work`; do not modify `main` or create/switch branches unless explicitly requested.
- Preserve the protected API-key block exactly, including `$env:GEMINI_APIJ` and its existing error text.
- Do not add ACL, ownership, or file-permission operations.
- Do not claim runtime success until tested against the actual Windows PowerShell script and live provider endpoints.

## Current implementation checkpoint

The provider separation and native Antigravity `googleSearch` wiring were committed to `work` in commit `0d38abf4f3d43a63a52b815643d7969cc0375c2c`. The updated file blob was verified on GitHub. Runtime behavior has not yet been validated; the checklist above records the remaining tests.


## New TODO — Windows OS integration tools (PLAN ONLY; do not implement yet)

Goal: give Mira useful, native Windows capabilities for assisting with operating-system setup and selected GUI tasks, without turning `gemma.ps1` into a large monolithic script.

### Architecture and packaging

- [ ] Design a small, modular Windows-tools layer kept outside the main chat script (for example, a `gemma/windows-tools/` folder with focused helper scripts/modules).
- [ ] Keep `gemma.ps1` limited to tool declarations, dispatch, permission checks, and result handling; load helper code only when needed.
- [ ] Prefer PowerShell and .NET/Win32 APIs for Windows-native operations. Use standalone `.cmd`/batch or VBScript helpers only when they provide a clear compatibility or GUI advantage; avoid adding multiple scripting languages without a concrete need.
- [ ] Define a stable input/output contract for helpers (structured parameters and structured results, preferably JSON), with clear error and cancellation reporting.
- [ ] Make the tools Windows-version-aware and check capability availability before invoking optional APIs.

### Candidate Windows capabilities

- [ ] Clipboard read through native Windows APIs (Win32) with Unicode text support; report unsupported formats cleanly.
- [ ] Optional clipboard write/set-text capability, separately permissioned from clipboard reading.
- [ ] GUI assistance for common setup flows: open the relevant Windows settings page, launch a selected application, and guide the user through steps.
- [ ] Window and process discovery: list visible windows and relevant process details; allow selecting a target window by title/process rather than guessing.
- [ ] Basic GUI automation using supported Windows UI Automation (UIA) patterns where practical; prefer semantic controls over fragile screen-coordinate clicks.
- [ ] Dialog assistance for file/folder selection and other standard Windows dialogs, with user confirmation before committing changes.
- [ ] Read-only system inspection useful for troubleshooting (Windows version/build, display information, environment variables, installed capabilities, and selected device/status information where APIs permit).
- [ ] Setup helpers for narrowly scoped, reversible configuration tasks, with a preview of the exact changes before applying them.

### Permission, privacy, and reliability requirements

- [ ] Every operation that changes system state must go through the existing permission model: ASK by default (Enter = Yes); AUTOPILOT only when the user explicitly enabled it.
- [ ] Separate read-only inspection from actions that modify settings, install software, change startup behavior, terminate processes, or write files.
- [ ] Never silently elevate privileges, bypass UAC, change ACLs/ownership, or alter file permissions. If an operation requires elevation, explain why and let the user handle the Windows prompt themselves.
- [ ] Clipboard access must be explicit and task-scoped: never poll or monitor the clipboard in the background, never read it automatically on startup, and warn that it may contain passwords, tokens, or other sensitive data.
- [ ] Show the target, intended action, and expected effect before GUI automation or system changes; support cancellation and timeouts.
- [ ] Avoid destructive defaults. Require stronger confirmation for deletion, process termination, registry edits, software installation, network changes, or security-setting changes.
- [ ] Log the requested operation and outcome without logging clipboard contents, credentials, or other sensitive payloads.
- [ ] Validate helper arguments, quote paths safely, avoid shell-string concatenation where structured process arguments are available, and return actionable errors.

### Implementation and test plan (future work)

- [ ] First produce a tool inventory and threat/safety review; do not start implementation until that design is reviewed.
- [ ] Prototype clipboard text read as an isolated helper and test Unicode, empty clipboard, locked clipboard, and unsupported formats.
- [ ] Prototype opening a Windows Settings page and a UIA-based interaction with one harmless standard control; include cancellation and timeout tests.
- [ ] Test on Windows PowerShell 5.1 and PowerShell 7 where supported; document Windows build/API requirements and fallbacks.
- [ ] Add integration tests for ASK approval, AUTOPILOT behavior, denied actions, helper failures, and sensitive-data redaction.
- [ ] Keep all helper code and tests separate from `gemma.ps1`; only add thin dispatch/registration glue to the main script after the helpers are proven.

Status: planning only. No Windows helper scripts, VBScript, CMD files, clipboard access, GUI automation, or operating-system changes have been implemented or executed as part of this TODO update.
